WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:08.006
Star Wars has been at the cutting edge of special&nbsp;effects.

00:00:08.006 --> 00:00:14.112
Puppets, prosthetics, animatronics, CGI... and now, thankfully, animatronics again.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:20.369
When the&nbsp;facial expressions go, or the limbs move in, it's almost like a little symphony you know.

00:00:20.457 --> 00:00:23.819
It all comes together in a very magical moment.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:30.540
To create the Star Wars world, we have to create&nbsp;monsters that inhabit these strange alien worlds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:36.850
Gustav's been making monsters for three decades,&nbsp;and he's taking a look back at his creations.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:38.588
I made many creatures...

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:39.457
Admiral Raddus

00:00:39.457 --> 00:00:40.519
Six Eyes

00:00:40.519 --> 00:00:41.930
L-O-S-D

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.040
Admiral Ackbar

00:00:43.097 --> 00:00:44.758
The head of Maz.

00:00:44.862 --> 00:00:48.240
Take us back to the beginning.       
Where did it all start? 

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:56.620
I remember&nbsp;vividly my dad taking me to see 'Return of the Jedi' in Tuschinski cinema in Amsterdam when I was&nbsp;six years old.

00:00:56.620 --> 00:00:59.291
That film sort of lit a spark in me.

00:00:59.291 --> 00:01:07.603
First of all I was just in awe, overwhelmed and&nbsp;it made me extremely curious how they crafted these films...

00:01:07.671 --> 00:01:10.629
How they can physically make something&nbsp;that realistic.

00:01:10.629 --> 00:01:17.082
I just took it all in, and I think that sort of slowly prepared me for my&nbsp;future career.

00:01:17.082 --> 00:01:19.830
I would read up on books, magazines.

00:01:19.993 --> 00:01:26.478
I started experimenting with rubber heads, hobby&nbsp;clay, plaster, wood, anything...

00:01:26.478 --> 00:01:32.252
And put them in my room as ornaments, you know, I just kept churning them&nbsp;out, trying to perfect my style.

00:01:32.252 --> 00:01:33.808
So how does it work?

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:36.212
It's a lengthy process.

00:01:36.212 --> 00:01:42.453
From scripts it goes to&nbsp;the design department, and then that mould lands on my desk.

00:01:42.453 --> 00:01:46.543
I insert clay to create&nbsp;the skin.

00:01:46.543 --> 00:01:49.800
Within that, I build my animatronics.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.164
Every hair has to be perfect.

00:01:51.164 --> 00:01:53.882
The movement&nbsp;has to be near perfect.

00:01:53.882 --> 00:01:56.450
You can only do so much with the rubber skin.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.508
That sounds like&nbsp;a lot...

00:01:58.508 --> 00:02:02.734
When I take a creature on set, my anxiety levels are through the roof

00:02:02.734 --> 00:02:09.048
just because I know&nbsp;what potentially could go wrong and how much is riding on it so I'm just hoping it's good.

00:02:09.048 --> 00:02:11.408
And it&nbsp;goes beyond Star Wars...

00:02:11.661 --> 00:02:13.050
'Hitchhikers' I did Jeltz.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.763
'Charlie and the Chocolate Factory' I did the&nbsp;
squirrels that listen to nuts.

00:02:16.872 --> 00:02:20.099
And they asked me to&nbsp;build a swimming Oompa Loompa.

00:02:20.099 --> 00:02:25.553
I remember not being&nbsp;able to sleep because I was so nervous because&nbsp;mechanics, electronics and water don't mix.

00:02:25.901 --> 00:02:27.196
Did it&nbsp;work out okay?

00:02:27.448 --> 00:02:28.838
The moment comes that we're shooting it.

00:02:28.838 --> 00:02:30.393
Tim Burton says 'Action'.

00:02:30.393 --> 00:02:34.889
The kids run in, run to the&nbsp;tank, and they say 'yep, release the Oompa Loompa.'

00:02:34.889 --> 00:02:38.541
'Switch it on, swim through frame.' He says, 'Cut, we got&nbsp;it.'

00:02:38.541 --> 00:02:41.863
That was one of them moments that I felt like I was walking on air.

00:02:41.863 --> 00:02:45.182
Gustav's done his fair&nbsp;share of creepy horrors too...

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:50.669
There was this small Finnish film I did and the creature we built - that&nbsp;was very prominent in the film.

00:02:50.669 --> 00:02:57.883
It was almost like a throwback to the 80s, you know, we're on the&nbsp;floor with rods and doing everything in our power to bring this thing to life.

00:02:57.955 --> 00:03:00.108
The blinks are still&nbsp;linked up.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.209
You can hear the buzzing noise.

00:03:02.582 --> 00:03:08.544
There was a mechanical jaw and a mechanical&nbsp;tongue, which was a nice little extra.

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:13.577
So after 30 years in the job, what's the secret?

00:03:13.577 --> 00:03:17.914
It's all about replicating the organic movements that you see in nature.

00:03:17.914 --> 00:03:19.841
That's the art of&nbsp;animatronics. 

00:03:19.841 --> 00:03:24.849
It's breathing life into a rubber skin to see one of your creations&nbsp;on a big screen.

00:03:24.849 --> 00:03:26.280
That's what you do it for.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:32.640
[Music]

